1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,465
Your app talks to a server
you'll never see.

2
00:00:02,705 --> 00:00:07,394
What exactly is it saying? And why do you
never need to know how that server works?

3
00:00:07,634 --> 00:00:11,843
That's an API. By the end of this
video, you'll read one, run one,

4
00:00:11,843 --> 00:00:14,266
and spot the mistakes that break
them.

5
00:00:14,716 --> 00:00:16,573
Here's the plan, in
five parts.

6
00:00:16,813 --> 00:00:21,476
One, the contract. Two, a request, line by
line. Three, the server side.

7
00:00:21,716 --> 00:00:25,863
Four, who calls APIs. And five, the
mistakes that break them.

8
00:00:27,863 --> 00:00:31,837
API stands for Application
Programming Interface. Strip

9
00:00:31,837 --> 00:00:34,582
away the acronym, and it's a
contract.

10
00:00:34,822 --> 00:00:37,954
One program asks another
program to do something,

11
00:00:37,954 --> 00:00:40,510
without knowing how it gets
done inside.

12
00:00:40,750 --> 00:00:44,921
The handbook's glossary puts it
the same way: a defined contract,

13
00:00:44,921 --> 00:00:47,488
with no need to know the
implementation.

14
00:00:47,938 --> 00:00:51,198
Think of a wall socket. You
plug in a lamp, and you know

15
00:00:51,198 --> 00:00:53,061
nothing about the power
station.

16
00:00:53,301 --> 00:00:55,464
The shape of the socket is
the promise.

17
00:00:55,914 --> 00:00:59,061
Or think of a restaurant. You
read the menu, you tell the

18
00:00:59,061 --> 00:01:01,655
waiter what you want, and the
kitchen cooks it.

19
00:01:01,895 --> 00:01:05,227
You never see the kitchen.
It can swap chefs, change

20
00:01:05,227 --> 00:01:07,469
the stove, even rewrite the
recipe.

21
00:01:07,709 --> 00:01:11,135
As long as the dish matches the
menu, you never notice.

22
00:01:11,375 --> 00:01:15,502
That's the whole value of an
API. Both sides can change

23
00:01:15,502 --> 00:01:18,878
independently, as long as the
contract holds.

24
00:01:20,878 --> 00:01:25,699
This handbook focuses on web
APIs. A client sends an HTTP

25
00:01:25,699 --> 00:01:29,421
request, and a server sends back
a response.

26
00:01:29,661 --> 00:01:33,231
The client is anything that
starts the conversation. A

27
00:01:33,231 --> 00:01:36,405
browser, a phone app, another
service, a script.

28
00:01:36,645 --> 00:01:42,182
MDN puts it plainly: the client always
initiates. The server never does.

29
00:01:42,632 --> 00:01:46,722
Here's a real request.
GET /users/42.

30
00:01:46,962 --> 00:01:52,019
GET is the method. It says what you want
to do. The path says which thing you want.

31
00:01:52,259 --> 00:01:58,129
Headers add context. Accept says you want
JSON. Authorization says who you are.

32
00:01:58,579 --> 00:02:03,173
Every HTTP message, request or
response, has the same shape: a

33
00:02:03,173 --> 00:02:07,396
start line, headers, an empty line,
and an optional body.

34
00:02:07,846 --> 00:02:12,005
The server answers with a status
code. 200 means OK.

35
00:02:12,245 --> 00:02:16,303
Then comes the body, in JSON. An
id, a name, an email.

36
00:02:16,543 --> 00:02:20,632
Nothing in this response says
whether the data came from

37
00:02:20,632 --> 00:02:25,087
PostgreSQL, MongoDB, or a plain
dictionary. That's the point.

38
00:02:25,537 --> 00:02:31,536
Status codes come in families. 2xx
mean success. 4xx mean the caller

39
00:02:31,536 --> 00:02:35,065
made a mistake. 5xx mean the
server did.

40
00:02:35,515 --> 00:02:40,754
One more thing from MDN. HTTP is
stateless. Every request stands alone, so

41
00:02:40,754 --> 00:02:46,559
the server doesn't remember the last one
unless you send something that identifies

42
00:02:46,559 --> 00:02:47,504
you.

43
00:02:49,504 --> 00:02:53,866
Now zoom out. Here is the whole trip,
drawn in the handbook as a sequence

44
00:02:53,866 --> 00:02:54,783
diagram.

45
00:02:55,023 --> 00:02:59,717
The client sends a request. The API
matches the route and validates it.

46
00:02:59,957 --> 00:03:03,518
Server logic fetches data
from the database, and the

47
00:03:03,518 --> 00:03:05,847
result travels back as a
response.

48
00:03:06,087 --> 00:03:09,697
The client only ever depends
on the API on the left.

49
00:03:09,697 --> 00:03:12,612
Everything to the right is
free to change.

50
00:03:13,062 --> 00:03:17,774
Now the other side. This is a
complete API endpoint, written in

51
00:03:17,774 --> 00:03:21,439
FastAPI, the framework this
handbook builds with.

52
00:03:21,679 --> 00:03:27,218
One decorator maps GET
/users/{user_id} to a Python function.

53
00:03:27,458 --> 00:03:33,164
The function looks the user up. If there's
no such user, it raises a 404.

54
00:03:33,404 --> 00:03:37,922
And because user id is typed as
an integer, FastAPI rejects

55
00:03:37,922 --> 00:03:41,062
anything else before your code
even runs.

56
00:03:41,512 --> 00:03:43,718
Run it, and ask for
user 42.

57
00:03:43,958 --> 00:03:46,982
You get the status line, the
headers, and the JSON.

58
00:03:47,222 --> 00:03:51,840
Ask for a user that doesn't
exist, and you get a 404. Ask

59
00:03:51,840 --> 00:03:54,999
for the word banana, and you
get a 422.

60
00:03:55,449 --> 00:03:59,185
Today that dictionary is fake.
Next week it could be

61
00:03:59,185 --> 00:04:02,130
PostgreSQL, or a call to
another service.

62
00:04:02,370 --> 00:04:06,741
Not one line the client
depends on changes. Same URL,

63
00:04:06,741 --> 00:04:09,628
same JSON shape, same status
codes.

64
00:04:09,868 --> 00:04:11,937
That is the contract
doing its job.

65
00:04:13,937 --> 00:04:16,396
Who calls APIs? More
than you think.

66
00:04:16,636 --> 00:04:20,449
Your weather app. The sign in
with Google button. A payment

67
00:04:20,449 --> 00:04:22,388
form. Another backend
service.

68
00:04:22,628 --> 00:04:28,740
And now, AI. Every call to a language
model through code is a web API: a

69
00:04:28,740 --> 00:04:33,155
POST request with a JSON body, and
JSON coming back.

70
00:04:33,605 --> 00:04:39,054
Same contract. The kitchen is just a very
large model you will never see inside.

71
00:04:39,294 --> 00:04:43,726
Postman's twenty twenty-five survey
of over fifty-seven hundred

72
00:04:43,726 --> 00:04:47,947
developers found that eighty-nine
percent use generative AI.

73
00:04:48,187 --> 00:04:53,424
But only twenty-four percent design their
APIs with AI agents in mind.

74
00:04:53,664 --> 00:04:58,023
The same survey found sixty-five
percent of organizations earn

75
00:04:58,023 --> 00:05:01,397
revenue from their APIs. Contracts
are products.

76
00:05:03,397 --> 00:05:10,839
Mistake one. Saying API when you mean REST
API. APIs also include function signatures

77
00:05:10,839 --> 00:05:17,230
and operating system calls. REST is one
architectural style for web APIs.

78
00:05:17,680 --> 00:05:22,918
Mistake two. Copying your database columns
straight into your responses. Rename a

79
00:05:22,918 --> 00:05:28,091
column, and every client breaks. Design
the contract first, then the code behind

80
00:05:28,091 --> 00:05:29,026
it.

81
00:05:29,476 --> 00:05:34,256
Mistake three. Returning 200 OK
for everything, even errors. The

82
00:05:34,256 --> 00:05:37,543
status code is part of the
contract. Use it.

83
00:05:37,993 --> 00:05:44,703
Mistake four. Confusing the API with the
whole system, or with the user interface.

84
00:05:44,703 --> 00:05:50,186
A UI consumes an API. One API can serve a
website and a mobile app.

85
00:05:50,636 --> 00:05:55,357
And mistake five. Leaving the
contract undocumented. If callers

86
00:05:55,357 --> 00:06:00,228
have to reverse engineer your
behavior, it's not a contract. Part

87
00:06:00,228 --> 00:06:04,275
three of the handbook covers OpenAPI
for exactly this.

88
00:06:06,275 --> 00:06:07,244
Let's recap.

89
00:06:07,484 --> 00:06:10,794
An API is a contract
between two programs.

90
00:06:11,034 --> 00:06:15,232
A web API is an HTTP request
and a response.

91
00:06:15,472 --> 00:06:17,638
The server behind it is
free to change.

92
00:06:17,878 --> 00:06:20,936
Anyone can be the caller,
even an AI model.

93
00:06:21,176 --> 00:06:23,632
And your job is to protect
the contract.

94
00:06:24,082 --> 00:06:28,161
Next up, Part one of the handbook: how
the internet actually works.

95
00:06:28,400 --> 00:06:33,251
Before you go, try the chapter's
exercise: add a GET /users endpoint,

96
00:06:33,251 --> 00:06:37,470
and decide what it should return when
the database is empty.

97
00:06:37,710 --> 00:06:40,045
Subscribe, and I'll see you
in the next one.

